比洛说，我的部队筋疲力尽。盟军有突破缺口的危险。比洛本人状态糟糕透顶。像所有的，首先，将军们，他已经68岁了，他有严重的甲状腺问题，还有严重的动脉粥样硬化。你们当中有谁是这样吗？他没有某种致命疾病。我不这么认为。我认为其性质在于，直到你60多岁时，你才会成为那种最高指挥官类型的人。而在那个时候，人们的生活方式非常不健康，我想，可能吧。他们一直在喝很多优质葡萄酒，这并没有给他们带来什么好处。

103
00:45:17.000 --> 00:45:31.000
Budov says, my troops are exhausted. The Allies are in danger of breaking through the gaps. Budov himself is in a terrible state. Like all, first of all, General's, he's got 68, he's got massive thyroid
8日晚些时候，在与亨施交谈时，比洛开始收到前线的这些更多报告。盟军正在突破，他对亨施说，我的军队正在化为灰烬。我们面临着彻底的灾难。亨施对他说，好吧，听着，毛奇已经授权我对你说，如果你想撤退，由你决定。这是德国军队中第一次真正谈论撤退。所以这是一个具有里程碑意义的时刻。他们没有立即做出决定，但一夜之间，情绪变得越来越黯淡。

104
00:45:31.000 --> 00:45:51.000
problems and he's got severe arterioschlorosis. Is there any of you? He doesn't have a kind of fatal illness. I don't think so. I think because the nature of it is you don't become a sort of Supreme Commander-type person, till you're in your late 60s. And at that point, people have had a very unhealthy lifestyle, I think, probably. There's been drinking a lot of fine wines, and it hasn't done them any good.
比洛三次泪流满面，说，你知道的，我应付不了这个。就像，我压力太大了，我们要输了。我的人都要死了，诸如此类的事情。早上5点，他们得到空中侦察报告说法国人越来越近了。所以在早上9点，事实上是9点零2分，我们喜欢法医般的事实。亨施对比洛说，好吧，好吧，做决定吧，撤退。于是，神经基本上已经崩溃的比洛下达了第二军撤退的命令。现在亨施继续他在前线的驾驶之旅，这是德国军事史上最灾难性的驾驶。

105
00:45:51.000 --> 00:46:21.000
Late on the 8th, while he's talking to Hensch, Boulof starts to get these more reports in the front. The Allies are breaking through, and he says to Hensh, my army's being reduced to cinders. We're facing total catastrophe. And Hensch says to him, well, look, Malka has given me authority to say to you, if you want to retreat, it's your call. And this is the first time that anybody in the German army has really talked about retreating. So this is a kind of landmark moment. They don't make the decision straight away, but overnight, the mood gets bleaker and bleaker.
他向西行驶了50英里，来到亚历山大·冯·克拉克第一军的指挥部。这是一次可怕的旅程。道路上挤满了吓坏了的难民。到处都是精疲力竭、受伤的士兵。有大量的火炮。有伤员，你知道的，有躺在沟渠里的人等等。这完全是一个战区。在各个点上，他的车实际上受到了自己人的攻击，因为太混乱了。

106
00:46:21.000 --> 00:47:00.000
Three times, Buleoff breaks down and floods of tears, says, you know, I can't cope with this. Like, I'm too stressed, we're going to lose. My men are all going to die, all this kind of thing. And at 5 o'clock in the morning, they get reports from air reconnaissance that the French are coming ever closer. And so at 9 o'clock in the morning, at 9.02, in fact, we love a forensic fact. Hensh says to Boulof, okay, fine, make the call, retreat. So Boulof, whose nerve is basically snapped now, issues the order for his second army to retreat. So now Hensk continues his driving tour of the front, the most disastrous drive in German military history.
秩序已经崩溃。他抵达第一军指挥部。当他到达时，第一军的军官实际上相当自信。他们说，是的，英国人已经进来了，但我们认为我们能应付英国人。我们以前应付过他们。亨施说，不，不，不，第二军已经在撤退了。所以你们可能也需要撤退。第一军的军官惊呆了。他们像是在说，什么？我们认为我们能赢。他说，不，不，不，毛奇告诉我有权做出这些决定。你知道的，你们应该撤退。所以当他回到卢森堡时，

107
00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:24.000
He drives 50 miles west to the headquarters of Alexander von Clux's First Army. It's a terrible journey. The roads are clogged with terrified refugees. There are exhausted and wounded soldiers everywhere. There's loads of artillery. There are wounded, you know, there's kind of men lying in the ditches, whatnot. It's a complete war zone. And at various points, his car actually comes under attack from his own side because it's so confused.
决定其实已经做出了。当天晚些时候，法军推进到了第二军曾经所在的地方，一个叫蒙米赖（Montmirai）的地方，当他们到达那里时，他们大吃一惊，因为那里什么都没有。冯·比洛的第二军已经消失了。在他们身后，他们留下了散落着垃圾的田野，包括数量惊人的空酒瓶，这可能说明了问题。德国人在战线后方度过了一段美好的时光。第二天，即10日，更西边的故事也是如此。所以马克斯·黑斯廷斯引用了一个名叫保罗·兰蒂尔（Paul Lantier）的炮手的话，他在我们上次谈到的阿登地区的凡尔登大屠杀中幸存下来，这个叫兰蒂尔的部队醒来后发现这真的很奇怪。鸟儿在歌唱，阳光在照耀，没有大炮，一队法国步兵走过，这个单位行进中的上校大喊，

108
00:47:24.000 --> 00:47:54.000
And the order is broken down. He arrives at First Army headquarters. When he gets there, the first army officers are actually quite. confident. They say, yeah, the British should come in, but we reckon we can handle the British. We've handled them before. And Hensh says, no, no, no, the second army is already retreating. So you probably need to fall back too. And the first army officers are stunned. They're like, what? We think we can win this. And he says, no, no, no, Malka has told me I've got authority to make these decisions. You know, you should pull back. So by the time he goes back to Luxembourg,
我们赢了。德国人在全线撤退。兰蒂尔事后说，消息口口相传，让我们欣喜若狂。胜利，胜利，而我们原本是如此不抱希望。所以德国人崩溃了。法国人赢了。对法国人来说，这成了一个绝对传奇的时刻。它获得了一种神圣的地位，被称为“马恩河上的奇迹”。而真正创造这个词的人，不幸的是在年底时，是一个极右翼、反犹太主义作家，名叫莫里斯·巴雷斯（Maurice Bages）。

109
00:47:54.000 --> 00:48:33.000
the decision has actually been made. And later that day, the French advanced on where the Second Army had been, a place called Montmirai, and they're astounded when they get there because there's nothing there. Von Bulow's Second Army has vanished. In their wake, they've left fields of strewn with rubbish, including an unbelievable quantity of empty wine bottles, which probably tells its own story. The Germans have had a fine old time in the back of the battle. The next day, the 10th, is the same story further west. So Max Hastings quotes a gunner called Paul Lantier who had survived the slaughter at Verton
他基本上说，马恩河上的奇迹，你知道的，你在蒙斯天使的番外中谈到的这个超自然元素，对法国那些非常右翼的极右翼作家来说非常重要。他说这是，

110
00:48:33.000 --> 00:48:51.000
in the Ardenne that we talked about last time this bloat Lantier's unit they wake up and they just find it's really weird. The birds are singing the sun is shining, there's no guns and a load of French infantry march passed and the colonel of this unit marching past shouts
我将马恩河上的奇迹与——我引用他的话——永恒的法兰西奇迹、圣女贞德的奇迹联系起来。那种认为在绝境中上帝会以某种方式干预并拯救法国免受侵略者侵害的想法。我的意思是，实际上，当然，真正值得赞扬的人是，

111
00:48:51.000 --> 00:49:29.000
we've won. The Germans are retreating all across the line. Nantier said afterwards the news as it passed from mouth to mouth shook us with joy. Victory, victory, when we were so far from expecting it. So the Germans have cracked. The French have won. And for the French, this becomes an absolutely legendary moment. It achieves a kind of sacred status as the miracle on the man. And the guy who actually coined that expression, unfortunately, at the end of the year, was an ultra-nationalist, anti-Semitic writer called Maurice Bages.
A，法国士兵，B，加利埃尼和霞飞，他们在关键时刻没有像他们的继任者在1940年那样惊慌失措。特别是霞飞，当他坐在那里吃着他那顿丰盛的午餐时，他选择了正确的反击时机。我的意思是，他对时机的把握是完美的。但如果你阅读关于马恩河的大多数记载，实际上大多数历史学家对法军做对了什么不太感兴趣，而对德军做错了什么更感兴趣。你可以责怪个别将军改变了计划，但我认为大多数历史学家会说真正的问题实际上在于整个概念，施里芬计划（The Schleifen plan），施里芬计划（Schleifens），施里芬和毛奇计划。这个为期六周的时间表是如此不切实际，以至于除非盟军惊慌失措并失去理智，否则它几乎不可能奏效。

112
00:49:29.000 --> 00:49:44.000
And he basically said the miracle on the Marn, you know, this element that you talked about in the angels of Mont's bonus, the supernatural, very important to sort of very right-wing ultra-nationalist writers in France. And he said this is,
基本上，如果盟军保持冷静，德国人的这台庞然大物最终总会散架。那我能问一下，是不是有一种感觉，德军对这个计划的执着意味着他们在临场应变方面不如原本那样灵活了？这是一个有趣的问题，因为，当然，当他们开始临场应变时，事情就出错。当他们开始改变计划时，我认为事实的真相是，德国的计划诞生于一个真正糟糕的战略地位，那就是你被夹在法国和俄罗斯之间。所以他们可能是对的，唯一的——我的意思是，我想他们本可以坐视不管，进行一场防守战，等待盟军的进攻，但是我的意思是谁愿意在两线作战中遭到进攻呢？我认为他们在进攻战中这可能是他们取胜的唯一途径，但这只是一场巨大无比的赌博。我的意思是，如果他们，你知道的，如果他们坚持最初的计划而不是即兴发挥，会不会效果更好？但即兴发挥是由这样一个事实强迫他们的：

113
00:49:44.000 --> 00:50:03.000
I associate the miracle in the Marn with, and I quote, the eternal French miracle, the miracle of Joan of Arc. The idea that, you know, in extremists, somehow God will intervene and he will save France from the invaders. I mean, actually, of course, the people who really deserve the credit,
这个计划根本无法坚持下去。是的，我认为在某种程度上你是完全正确的，

114
00:50:03.000 --> 00:50:51.000
A, the French soldiers, and B, Galiini and Jophe, who in the crucial moment had not panicked, as their successors panicked in 1940. And Jophe, in particular, when he's sitting there eating his enormous lunches, he has chosen the right moment to strike back. I mean, his grasp of timing was perfect. But if you read most accounts of them on, actually most historians are less interested in what the French got right and more interested in what the Germans got wrong. You could blame the individual generals for changing their plan, but I think most historians would say the real problem is actually with the whole concept, the Schleifen plan, the Schlefenm, the Schlef and Molka plan. This idea of the six-week timetable was so unrealistic that there's probably no way that it could have worked unless the Allies panicked and lost their nerve.
他们不得不开始即兴发挥。而且我认为也许还有一个心理因素，回到你的问题。我想如果你被告知，我们必须在六周内完成这个，这是唯一能做到的计划，当计划开始出错时，就会产生巨大的恐慌，是的。

115
00:50:51.000 --> 00:51:34.000
that basically if the allies kept their call, the wheels were always going to come off, the German juggernaut. So can I ask, is there a sense perhaps that the commitment on the German part to this plan means that they are less able to kind of think on their feet than they would otherwise have been? It's an interesting question because, of course, when they do think on their feet, it goes wrong. When they start to change the plan, I think that the truth of the matter is that the German plan is born of a really poor, strategic position, which is your sandwich between France and Russia. And so they're probably right that the only, I mean, I suppose they could have just sat and done a defensive war and waited to be attacked by the Allies, but I mean,
嗯，这正是发生的事情。所以我认为德国人在心理上受到的打击可能比盟军在边界战役撤退时受到的打击要大得多。嗯，英国人喜欢撤退，

116
00:51:34.000 --> 00:51:59.000
who wants to be attacked on two fronts? I think they were probably right that in an offensive war, this was the only way they could win, but it's just a massive, massive gamble. I mean, if they, you know, would it have worked better if they had stuck to the original and not improvised? But the improvisations are forced on them by the fact that. the plan is simply impossible to stick to. Yeah, I think to some extent you're absolutely right,
不是吗？你会喜欢往后退，因为你知道那样你会赢。好吧，你先输，然后你撤退，然后你赢。没错。这就是所有英国历史的故事。因为你所有的那些记录，

117
00:51:59.000 --> 00:52:14.000
that they have to start improvising. And I think as well maybe there is a psychological element to go back to your question. I think if you have been told, we have to do this in six weeks and this is the only plan that will do it, when the plan starts to go wrong, massive panic, yeah.
那些日记和信件，德国军官在交谈时说，你知道的，人们在哭泣，他们崩溃了，他们糊涂了，所有这些类的事情。我的意思是，这是一个关键的事情，我认为从长远来看，这在德国历史上非常非常重要。

118
00:52:14.000 --> 00:52:28.000
Well, this is exactly what happens. So I think the Germans are probably psychologically much more shaken by their retreat from the Battle of the Marne than the Allies were by their retreat from the Battle of the Frontiers. Well, the British love a retreat,
在这个时候，他们并不认为自己正在失败。马恩河战役中的很多人认为他们正在赢，或者至少他们守住了阵地。当他们接到向后撤退的命令时，他们并不觉得这是一支被打败的军队。所以我认为实际上正是在这里，在战争爆发一个月后，你萌生了一种非常有害的政治迷因的起源，我想，这就是我们被背叛的想法。我们被出卖了。好吧，所以是背后捅刀？我们被高层的悲观主义者在背后捅了刀子。现在，在这个时候，他们并没有用如此明确的术语来谈论，而且他们显然

119
00:52:28.000 --> 00:52:41.000
don't they? You'd be like going backwards because you know you're going to win that way. Well, you lose first, and then you retreat and then you win. Exactly. That's the story of all British history. Because all the accounts
没有在谈论，你知道的，共产主义者、犹太人以及他们在第一次世界大战结束时指责的其他人。但确实有这种感觉：我们本该赢的。你知道的，我们理应获得胜利。出于某种原因，它被剥夺了。多米尼克，凯撒大帝在这其中扮演了什么角色？他在和毛奇厮混吗？凯撒在后面闲逛。

120
00:52:41.000 --> 00:52:55.000
that you have, the sort of diaries and letters, German officers are talking, and they say, you know, people are crying, they're devastated, they're confused, all of this kind of thing. I mean, here's a key thing, and I think it's really, really important in the long run in German history.
显然，他和任何人一样感到困惑、迷茫和愤怒，尽管他不像冯·毛奇那样彻底崩溃，因为冯·毛奇正如我们所说，有病而且抑郁，基本上总是处于崩溃的边缘，我的意思是，他把这整件事看得极其糟糕。事实上，在战役期间，在这个时期，他一直在给他的妻子写这些非同寻常的信。

121
00:52:55.000 --> 00:53:30.000
At this point, they didn't think they were losing. A lot of people in the Battle of the Month thought they were winning, or that at least they were holding their own. They didn't feel like a beaten army when they got the orders to go backwards. So I think it's actually here, a month into the war, that you have in embryo the origin of a very poisonous political meme, I suppose, which is the idea we've been betrayed. We've been cheated. Okay, so stab in the back? We've been stabbed in the back by defeatists in high places. Now, at this point, they're not talking in such explicit terms, and they're obviously
我找不到词语来形容过去几天压在我肩上的沉重责任感。我们目前处境的骇人困难像一道黑色的帘幕一样挂在我的眼前，透过它我什么也看不到。全世界都在结盟与我们为敌。似乎每个国家都一心要彻底摧毁德国。它们非常顾影自怜，但实际上也有很多负罪感。他说，一想到有多少德国人死去，我就感到恶心。恐怖经常向我袭来，当我想到这一点时，我觉得我必须对这场恐怖负责。

122
00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:47.000
not talking about, you know, communists, Jews, all the rest that they blame at the end of the First World War. But there is this sense, we should have won. You know, we deserved victory. And for some reason, it has been denied us. And Dominic, where is the Kaiser in all this? Is he hanging out with Molka? The Kaiser's hanging around at the back.
我的意思是，作为你的最高指挥官，这很不理想。是的，这不太好吧？当人们去他在卢森堡的指挥部时，他们对所看到的一切感到震惊。我的意思是，一次又一次，人们说，天哪，他是个废人。他是一个心碎的人。有一个叫卡尔·艾内姆（Carl Einem）的将军出现了。毛奇只是对他说，天哪，你知道的，怎么会发生这种事？艾内姆对他发了脾气，说，如果你不知道，谁知道？

123
00:53:47.000 --> 00:54:13.000
Obviously, he's as baffled. and as confused and as angry as anybody, although he's not quite as devastated as von Malka is, because von Malka, who as we've said is sickly and depressive and basically always on the brink of a breakdown, I mean, he's really taken this whole business incredibly badly. And in fact, during the battle, during this period, he's been writing these extraordinary letters to his wife.
你应该比任何人都清楚。你怎么能一直呆在卢森堡，任由领导权的缰绳从你手中滑落？所以在马恩河战役几天后，即9月14日，毛奇真的开始崩溃了。他在房间里以非常激动的姿态踱来踱去，通过牙缝吹口哨。基本上，消息传回了柏林。人们对凯撒说，你得解雇他。他必须走。他完全失控了。

124
00:54:13.000 --> 00:54:46.000
I cannot find the words to describe the crushing burden of responsibility that's weighed on my shoulders in the last few days. The appalling difficulties of our present situation hang before my eyes like a dark curtain through which I can see nothing. The whole world is in league against us. It would seem that every country is bent on destroying Germany once and for all. They're very self-pitying, but also there's a lot of guilt, actually. He says I feel sick to think of how many Germans have died. Often terror overcomes me when I think of this, and I have the feeling that I must answer for this horror.
所以在14日晚上，凯撒任命了一名新的最高指挥官，一名新的总参谋长。他是一个冷酷得多的人物。他就是普鲁士战争大臣埃里希·冯·法金汉（Eric von Falkenhyme）。这是一个好名字。我的意思是，我们提到过这个。就像猎鹰俯冲下来一样。没错。现在，他来自一个普鲁士土地拥有者家庭，这意味着他是一个容克（Juncker）贵族。

125
00:54:46.000 --> 00:55:10.000
I mean, this is very sub-ideal from your Supreme Commander. Yeah, that's not great, is it? And when people go to his headquarters in Luxembourg, they're horrified by what they find. I mean, again and again, people say, God, he's a wreck. He's a broken man. There's one general who turns up called Carl Einem. And Malka just says to him, my God, you know, how could this have happened? And Einem loses his temper with him and says, well, if you don't know, who knows?
我的意思是，这对你的最高指挥官来说是非常不理想的。是的，这不太好，对吧？当人们去他在卢森堡的总部时，他们对所看到的一切感到震惊。我的意思是，一次又一次，人们说，天哪，他已经崩溃了。他是一个心碎的人。有个叫卡尔·艾内姆的将军出现了。毛奇对他说，天哪，你知道的，这怎么可能发生？艾内姆对他发脾气说，好吧，如果你不知道，谁知道呢？

126
00:55:10.000 --> 00:55:37.000
You should know better than anybody. How could you have remained in Luxembourg and allowed the reins of leadership to slip from your hands? So a few days after the Battle of the Marne, 14th of September, Malka really starts to lose it. He's sort of pacing up and down the room in a very agitated way, whistling through his teeth. And basically, word gets back to Berlin. People say to the Kaiser, you've got to fire him. He's got to go. He's lost it completely.
你本该比任何人都清楚。你怎么能一直呆在卢森堡，眼睁睁看着领导权从你手中溜走呢？所以在马恩河战役几天后，即9月14日，毛奇真的开始崩溃了。他在房间里焦躁不安地踱来踱去，从牙缝里吹着口哨。基本上，消息传回了柏林。人们对德皇说，你必须解雇他。他必须走。他已经彻底崩溃了。

